  "content": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "Pre-calculus functions — rational, piecewise, exponential, and logarithmic — with composition, inverses, and detailed graph analysis.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Rational function",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\dfrac{P(x)}{Q(x)} \\) — vertical asymptotes where \\(Q=0\\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Horizontal asymptote",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "compare degrees of \\( P \\) and \\( Q \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Piecewise",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "different rules on different intervals",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Even / odd",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( f(-x)=f(x) \\) / \\( f(-x)=-f(x) \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 3.A",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "Find the horizontal asymptote of \\( f(x)=\\dfrac{3x^2+1}{x^2-4} \\). <p>Both numerator and denominator have degree 2; ratio of leading coefficients: \\( y=\\dfrac{3}{1}=3 \\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "explorer",
        "title": {
          "en": "⊙ Explorer · Rational function asymptotes",
          "ar": null
        },
        "canvasId": "act2RatCanvas",
        "ariaLabel": {
          "en": "A rational function f of x equals 2 over x minus p plus q, with adjustable vertical and horizontal asymptotes",
          "ar": null
        },
        "dataPanel": {
          "btnId": "act2RatDataBtn",
          "panelId": "act2RatDataPanel",
          "descId": "act2RatDataDesc",
          "rowsId": "act2RatDataRows",
          "caption": {
            "en": "Sampled points on the current curve",
            "ar": null
          },
          "columns": [
            {
              "en": "x",
              "ar": null
            },
            {
              "en": "y",
              "ar": null
            }
          ]
        },
        "controls": [
          {
            "id": "act2RatP",
            "valueDisplayId": "act2RatPlab",
            "label": {
              "en": "vertical asymptote <b>p</b>:",
              "ar": null
            },
            "ariaLabel": {
              "en": "p",
              "ar": null
            },
            "min": -5,
            "max": 5,
            "value": 1,
            "step": 1
          },
          {
            "id": "act2RatQ",
            "valueDisplayId": "act2RatQlab",
            "label": {
              "en": "horizontal asymptote <b>q</b>:",
              "ar": null
            },
            "ariaLabel": {
              "en": "q",
              "ar": null
            },
            "min": -5,
            "max": 5,
            "value": 0,
            "step": 1
          }
        ],
        "readouts": [
          {
            "id": "act2VAsym",
            "label": {
              "en": "vertical asymptote",
              "ar": null
            },
            "colorClass": "indigo"
          },
          {
            "id": "act2HAsym",
            "label": {
              "en": "horizontal asymptote",
              "ar": null
            },
            "colorClass": "amber"
          }
        ],
        "note": {
          "en": "\\(f(x)=\\dfrac{2}{x-p}+q\\) — the denominator vanishing at \\(x=p\\) makes the vertical asymptote; as \\(x\\to\\pm\\infty\\), \\(f(x)\\to q\\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "● Piecewise & Recursive Functions",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "A <strong>piecewise function</strong> uses different rules for different intervals. A <strong>recursive function</strong> is defined by its previous terms: e.g. \\(a_1 = 2\\), \\(a_n = 3a_{n-1} - 1\\). <strong>Parametric equations</strong> express \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) as separate functions of a parameter \\(t\\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example — Parametric to Cartesian",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "Eliminate \\(t\\) from \\(x = t+2\\), \\(y = t^2 - 1\\). <p>From \\(x = t+2\\): \\(t = x-2\\). Substitute: \\(y = (x-2)^2 - 1 = x^2 - 4x + 3\\). Parabola.</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "A function is defined piecewise: \\(f(x) = x^2\\) for \\(x < 0\\), and \\(f(x) = 2x+1\\) for \\(x \\geq 0\\). Find \\(f(-3) + f(2)\\).",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "\\(f(-3) = (-3)^2 = 9\\). \\(f(2) = 2(2)+1 = 5\\). Sum \\(= 14\\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  "quizWidget": {
    "enabled": true
  }
}
